"""Every code listing of the SEN0401 chapter 14 deck 2.0.0 (Second-Layer Applications; a NEW file, the deck 1.1.0 and its examples_v1_0_0.py stay unchanged) and the source of the chapter-code
manifest chapter_code_ch14_v1_0_0.json that the page's chapter-code tab reads. One entry per code slide, in SLIDE ORDER. A listing is a list of GROUPS; a group is a few single-line
statements and the plain-language note that explains them (the deck standard asks for an explanation beside every code box). Every statement is ONE line, at most 56 characters, so that a code
line never wraps (deck standard D12). The last row of a listing is an expression (`final`, which is also in `show`): the slide prints its value under it, the only REPL text of the slide;
the full result of the example is drawn as a chart or a table beside the listing from the recorded `show` values (examples_run_v2_0_0.py), never typed.
Every example uses only what the chapter wheel of the page holds (sen0401_ch14_lib-1.0.0: the chapter library, the saved BOLT texts and the book chapter, and the single recorded run of
Bitcoin Core 31.1 and five lnd 0.21.3-beta nodes on private regtest chains): none reads a file outside the wheel, so each runs in the browser as it runs here. The wheel does not hold the BOLT 4
onion test file or the mempool.space statistics, so the old deck's onion rebuild and graph figures are not used. L is the chapter library, R the recorded run, seen the passage finder.
Names of the setup: sys, math, hashlib, datetime, L, R, seen (the same names as the deck 1.1.0 setup, so its deck check can replay these listings)."""
__version__ = "2.0.0"

